BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[Department of Aeronautics and Astronautics / Earth & Planetary Sc
 iences] Planetary Science and Exploration Seminar\, Catherine Johnson  - "
 Mercury’s Enigmatic Magnetic Field"
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261104T123000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261104T132000
DTSTAMP:20261103T195903Z
UID:stanford_campus-localist-54347058940584-54347069831914@local-events
DESCRIPTION:Mercury’s interior structure and its magnetic field are uniq
 ue among the terrestrial planets and moons of the Solar System. Its large 
 metallic core (~83% of the planetary radius) formed under highly reducing 
 conditions\, requiring a core composition that includes substantial silico
 n and some\, but more limited\, sulfur and carbon. Mercury is also the onl
 y planet other than Earth to have both core and crustal magnetic fields. T
 he core field constrains the present-day dynamo and crustal magnetization
 \n\nAges: All ages\nLast synced: 2026-11-03 11:59 PST
LOCATION:Mitchell Earth Sciences
END:VEVENT
